PAGES = [
dict(slug="how-much-does-a-marketing-agency-cost", title="How Much Does a Marketing Agency Cost? | Extra Medium",
     hero="collage-work",
     q="How much does a marketing agency cost?",
     desc="What businesses actually pay a marketing agency, what drives the number up or down, what cheap really costs, and how retainers, projects and fractional leadership compare.",
     answer="A marketing agency typically charges a monthly retainer, and the honest answer is that the range is wide because the work is: a narrow, well-defined scope costs far less than a full growth function. What you are really buying is seniority, scope and speed &mdash; and the cheapest quote is usually the most expensive mistake.",
     sections=[
       ("What actually drives the number", "<p>Four things set the price. <b>Scope</b>: one channel run well costs a fraction of a full function &mdash; strategy, creative, media, content and reporting under one roof. <b>Seniority</b>: you are paying for the judgment of the people on your account, and a senior operator costs more per hour than a junior with a checklist. <b>Speed</b>: launches and turnarounds compress timelines, and compressed timelines cost. <b>Strategy</b>: execution-only engagements look cheaper until you realize nobody is deciding what the execution is for. One more thing that is not in the fee: ad spend. The retainer buys the people and the thinking; the media budget is yours and goes to the platforms.</p>"),
       ("What cheap really costs", "<p>A cheap quote usually buys junior hours, template work and activity that was never aimed at anything. The invoice is small; the damage is not. It shows up as a year of media spend pointed at the wrong audience, creative that has to be redone by the next firm, and a leadership team that concludes &ldquo;marketing doesn&rsquo;t work&rdquo; when what actually failed was the aiming. The most expensive line in most marketing budgets is never the agency fee. It is the spend that ran all year underneath a strategy nobody set.</p>"),
       ("Retainer, project, or a seat at the table", "<p>The three shapes buy different things. A <b>project</b> buys a defined outcome &mdash; a launch, a rebrand, a website. A <b>retainer</b> buys an ongoing function: the calendar run, the channels managed, the reporting rhythm kept. <b>Fractional leadership</b> buys direction itself, for companies whose real gap is that nobody senior is setting the strategy the other two shapes execute. Published market ranges give the shape of it: fractional CMO retainers typically run $3,000&ndash;$20,000 a month, while a full-time CMO typically costs $300K&ndash;$570K a year fully loaded &mdash; which is exactly why the fractional seat exists. Our breakdown of <a href=\"/fractional-cmo-cost/\">what a fractional CMO costs</a> goes deeper, and <a href=\"/fractional-cmo/\">the fractional CMO practice</a> explains how the seat works.</p>"),
     ],
     faqs=[
       ("Is ad spend included in the agency fee?", "Almost never. The retainer pays for strategy and the people who plan, create and manage the work; the media budget is separate and goes to the platforms. Any quote that blurs the two into one number deserves a second look."),
       ("Why do quotes for the same work vary so much?", "Because the work is not the same. Quotes differ on who actually does it, whether strategy is included, how much reporting and senior attention you get, and how much of the team is senior versus handed down. The deliverables list can look identical while the judgment behind it is not."),
       ("How do we start with Extra Medium?", "In writing, through the contact page. Tell us what you are trying to grow and where it feels stuck. The first conversation is specific: what we would do, in what order, and what it would cost, all before any work begins."),
     ],
     takeaways=[
       "You are not buying hours. You are buying seniority, scope and speed.",
       "The cheapest quote is usually the most expensive mistake.",
       "The agency fee is rarely the waste. The unaimed spend underneath it is.",
     ],
     keys=["marketing agency cost", "agency retainer", "marketing agency pricing", "fractional CMO cost", "project vs retainer", "ad spend"],
     iso="2026-11-15"),
dict(slug="why-is-my-marketing-not-working", title="Why Is My Marketing Not Working? | Extra Medium",
     hero="collage-about",
     q="Why is my marketing not working?",
     desc="The honest diagnosis list: no strategy, wrong audience, weak offer, no follow-through, vanity metrics, stopping too early, and how to tell which one is yours.",
     answer="Marketing usually fails for one of five reasons: there is no strategy underneath it, it is aimed at the wrong audience, the offer is weak, the follow-through stops early, or success is being measured in likes instead of revenue. The fix starts with diagnosis, not more activity &mdash; more of the wrong thing is just more.",
     sections=[
       ("The five usual suspects", "<p><b>No strategy.</b> Channels are running, posts are going out, ads are live &mdash; and nobody can say what it is all for, in one sentence. Activity without a decision underneath it is just noise with a budget. <b>Wrong audience.</b> The work is aimed at everyone, or at people who admire the product but cannot buy it. <b>A weak offer.</b> There is nothing concrete to say yes to, so interest has nowhere to go. <b>Follow-through.</b> Leads get answered late or never, and campaigns get killed at week three, right before compounding starts. <b>Measurement.</b> The dashboard celebrates reach and likes while revenue sits in a different report nobody opens.</p>"),
       ("How to tell which one is yours", "<p>Run five quick tests. Can you state the strategy in one sentence without naming a channel? If not, that is suspect one. Compare who engages with your marketing to who actually buys: if they are different people, that is suspect two. Read your offer as a stranger: is there a specific thing to accept, with a reason to accept it now? Mystery-shop your own lead flow and time the response: anything slower than the same business day is suspect four at work. Finally, look at the number your team checks every Monday. If it is not connected to revenue, suspect five is running the meeting.</p>"),
       ("Fix the diagnosis before the budget", "<p>The instinct when marketing fails is to spend more or switch channels. Both moves skip the only step that matters: finding out which of the five is broken. The repair order is the order above &mdash; strategy first, then audience, offer, follow-through, measurement &mdash; because each one makes the next one cheaper. This is the diagnosis-first sequence behind <a href=\"/services/\">how the studio works</a>, and it is also the core of the <a href=\"/fractional-cmo/\">fractional CMO seat</a>: a senior operator whose first job is to find the real constraint, not to sell you more activity.</p>"),
     ],
     faqs=[
       ("How long should we give marketing before judging it?", "Long enough to measure a real cycle. Most programs need a full quarter of consistent execution against one strategy before the verdict means anything. Judging at week three measures the launch, not the marketing."),
       ("Should we fire our agency and start over?", "Sometimes. But if the strategy was never set, the next agency inherits the same problem with a new logo on the reports. Diagnose first; the answer is often direction, not personnel."),
       ("How do we start with Extra Medium?", "In writing, through the contact page. Describe what you have been running and what it has produced. The first conversation is a diagnosis, not a pitch: which of the five suspects is yours, and what we would do about it first."),
     ],
     takeaways=[
       "More of the wrong thing is just more.",
       "Marketing rarely fails in the channels. It fails in the aiming.",
       "Diagnose before you spend. The order of the fix is the order of the problem.",
     ],
     keys=["marketing not working", "marketing strategy", "vanity metrics", "lead follow-up", "marketing diagnosis", "wrong audience"],
     iso="2026-11-15"),
dict(slug="marketing-agency-vs-freelancer", title="Marketing Agency vs. Freelancer | Extra Medium",
     hero="collage-work",
     q="Should I hire a marketing agency or a freelancer?",
     desc="When a freelancer is the right call, when an agency is, the hidden costs of each, and the hybrid most growing companies land on: fractional leadership over freelance hands.",
     answer="A freelancer is the right call when the job is one skill, well defined, and someone inside your business can direct it. An agency is the right call when you need several skills working from one strategy. The trap is hiring freelance hands when the thing you actually lack is direction.",
     sections=[
       ("When the freelancer wins", "<p>One skill, one defined deliverable, and a clear brief: a designer for the identity, a developer for the build, a writer for the campaign. A good freelancer is a specialist you rent by the job, and when the job is genuinely self-contained, paying agency overhead to get the same hands makes no sense. The condition that makes it work is direction. Somebody on your side has to know what good looks like, write the brief, and judge the result. If that somebody exists, freelance is often the sharpest buy in marketing.</p>"),
       ("When the agency wins, and what each really costs", "<p>The agency wins when the work needs several skills moving together under one strategy, or when continuity matters: campaigns that cannot pause because one person got busy, sick, or booked elsewhere. Now the hidden costs. Freelancers make <i>you</i> the agency: sourcing, briefing, coordinating and quality-checking five specialists is a real job, and it lands on whoever already has the fullest plate. Agencies carry the opposite tax: account layers between you and the people doing the work, and juniors learning on your budget. The fee is never the whole cost on either side; the question is which tax you would rather pay.</p>"),
       ("The hybrid most growing companies land on", "<p>Senior direction at the top, rented hands underneath. A fractional CMO sets the strategy, writes the briefs and judges the work, while freelancers or a studio execute the pieces. You pay leadership prices only for leadership, and execution prices only for execution &mdash; and the freelancers you already like usually get better, because somebody senior is finally aiming them. We wrote up the leadership-versus-execution distinction in <a href=\"/fractional-cmo-vs-agency/\">fractional CMO vs. marketing agency</a>, and the seat itself is described at <a href=\"/fractional-cmo/\">the fractional CMO practice</a>.</p>"),
     ],
     faqs=[
       ("Is a freelancer always cheaper than an agency?", "On the invoice, often. In total, it depends on your time. Freelance hands plus twenty hours a month of your own coordination, plus the rework when direction was missing, can cost more than the retainer you were avoiding."),
       ("What if a freelancer disappears mid-project?", "It happens, and it is the structural risk of the model: one person is a single point of failure. Protect yourself by owning the files, accounts and documentation from day one, so any competent replacement can pick the work up."),
       ("How do we start with Extra Medium?", "In writing, through the contact page. Tell us what you have been hiring out and where it keeps snagging. We will say plainly whether you need an agency, a freelancer with better direction, or the hybrid, and what we would do first."),
     ],
     takeaways=[
       "Hire hands when the job is defined. Hire direction when it is not.",
       "Freelancers make you the agency. Agencies can bury you in layers. Pick your tax.",
       "The hybrid works: senior direction on top, rented hands underneath.",
     ],
     keys=["marketing agency vs freelancer", "freelancer", "marketing agency", "fractional CMO", "marketing management", "hybrid marketing team"],
     iso="2026-11-15"),
dict(slug="how-to-choose-a-marketing-agency", title="How to Choose a Marketing Agency | Extra Medium",
     hero="collage-home",
     q="How do I choose the right marketing agency?",
     desc="The questions to ask, the red flags to walk away from, what a good first 90 days actually looks like, and why case-study theater misleads.",
     answer="Choose an agency the way you would hire a senior employee: ask who will actually do the work, how they decide what to do, and how they will report against revenue. Beware case-study theater &mdash; polished results from other people&rsquo;s budgets. A good agency can explain its first 90 days on your business before you sign anything.",
     sections=[
       ("The questions that separate operators from decks", "<p>Ask five. <b>Who, by name, works on our account, and how senior are they?</b> If the pitch team vanishes after signing, you have your answer. <b>What would you stop us doing?</b> An operator has a list; a salesperson has a menu. <b>How do you decide what to work on each month?</b> You are listening for a strategy, not a content calendar. <b>How do you report?</b> The answer should end at revenue, not impressions. <b>What do you need from us to succeed?</b> Good agencies name their dependencies honestly, because they have been burned by the same bottlenecks you are about to describe.</p>"),
       ("Red flags, including the shiny ones", "<p>Guaranteed rankings, guaranteed revenue, guaranteed anything: marketing has weather in it, and honest operators say so. Long lock-in contracts demanded before any diagnosis has happened. Reports that lead with reach and bury the money. And the subtlest one: <b>case-study theater</b>. A beautiful results slide tells you a budget was large, a market was kind, or a team three jobs ago did good work. It does not tell you what this team will do with your constraints. Ask what they would do in your first 90 days instead &mdash; specificity about your business beats trophies from somebody else&rsquo;s.</p>"),
       ("What a good first 90 days looks like", "<p>Days 1&ndash;30 are diagnosis: the numbers audited, the waste named, the quick leaks stopped, the strategy written down in plain language. Days 31&ndash;60 are the first proof: the highest-leverage work shipped, measured against the baseline the diagnosis set. Days 61&ndash;90 are rhythm: the calendar running, reporting tied to revenue, and the next quarter planned from evidence instead of hope. If an agency cannot narrate some version of that arc for your business in the first conversation, keep looking. You can see the standard we hold ourselves to in <a href=\"/work/\">the studio&rsquo;s work</a> and <a href=\"/services/\">how engagements are shaped</a>.</p>"),
     ],
     faqs=[
       ("Should an agency specialize in my industry?", "It helps less than pitch decks claim. Pattern recognition across industries often beats narrow experience, because the specialist may keep re-running the playbook your competitors also bought. Ask what transfers to your business and what they would need to learn."),
       ("How long a contract should I sign?", "Long enough to judge fairly: a quarter is a reasonable test of a real program. Be wary of long lock-ins demanded up front, before the agency has diagnosed anything. Commitment should grow with evidence, not precede it."),
       ("How do we start with Extra Medium?", "In writing, through the contact page. Ask us the five questions above, starting with who does the work. The first conversation is specific about your business, and if we are not the right fit we will say so and point you somewhere better."),
     ],
     takeaways=[
       "Hire an agency like a senior employee, not like a vendor.",
       "Case studies prove a budget existed. Ask about your first 90 days instead.",
       "A good agency can tell you what it would stop, not just what it would start.",
     ],
     keys=["choose a marketing agency", "agency red flags", "first 90 days", "case studies", "agency selection", "marketing agency"],
     iso="2026-11-15"),
dict(slug="small-business-marketing-mistakes", title="Small Business Marketing Mistakes | Extra Medium",
     hero="collage-about",
     q="What are the most common small business marketing mistakes?",
     desc="The recurring five: boosting posts instead of strategy, copying big brands, leading with no offer, never following up, and marketing only when sales dip.",
     answer="The same five, on repeat: boosting posts instead of running a strategy, copying what big brands do, leading with no real offer, never following up on the interest marketing creates, and only marketing when sales dip. None of them is a budget problem. All of them are fixable in the order they are listed.",
     sections=[
       ("Activity dressed up as strategy", "<p>Boosting a post feels like marketing: money goes out, numbers come back. But a boost just pays to distribute a post that never had a job, to an audience chosen by a button. Underneath it there is no decision about who this is for or what they should do next. The companion mistake is copying big brands. National companies run brand campaigns because they already have distribution, shelf space and budgets that buy reach by the ton. A local business copying the style without the scale gets the expense of brand advertising with none of the machinery that makes it pay.</p>"),
       ("No offer, and no follow-up", "<p>Most small business marketing points at nothing: no specific offer, no reason to act now, just a general hope that people remember the name. Then, when the marketing does work and someone raises a hand, the follow-up leaks it away &mdash; the inquiry answered tomorrow, the estimate sent next week, the lead left in an inbox. Interest decays in hours, not weeks. These two mistakes together mean businesses pay to create demand and then decline to collect it, which is why they are more expensive than any line in the ad budget.</p>"),
       ("Marketing only when sales dip", "<p>The feast-or-famine cycle: sales slow down, panic marketing starts, work arrives, marketing stops because everyone is busy, the pipeline quietly empties, sales slow down. Marketing has a lag &mdash; the customers you need in six weeks are decided by the presence you build now &mdash; so switching it on during the famine guarantees the famine lasts. A steady, modest presence beats heroic bursts every time. If your results feel random, the longer diagnosis in <a href=\"/why-is-my-marketing-not-working/\">why your marketing is not working</a> will sound familiar, and <a href=\"/services/\">the studio&rsquo;s Grow practice</a> is built around exactly this rhythm problem.</p>"),
     ],
     faqs=[
       ("We have almost no budget. What matters most?", "The cheapest fixes are the biggest ones: a concrete offer, same-day follow-up, and being easy to find locally cost little or nothing. Fix the leaks before buying more reach to pour into them."),
       ("Which mistake should we fix first?", "Follow-up. It is the only fix that makes every dollar you already spend work harder, starting today, with no new budget and no new tools."),
       ("How do we start with Extra Medium?", "In writing, through the contact page. Tell us which of the five sounds most like your business. The first conversation names the fix order for your situation, and what we would do in the first month."),
     ],
     takeaways=[
       "Boosting a post is paying to distribute a decision you never made.",
       "Interest decays in hours. Follow-up is marketing, not admin.",
       "Marketing only when sales dip guarantees the dip lasts.",
     ],
     keys=["small business marketing mistakes", "boosted posts", "lead follow-up", "marketing offer", "feast or famine marketing", "small business marketing"],
     iso="2026-11-15"),
dict(slug="how-local-businesses-get-more-customers", title="How Local Businesses Get More Customers | Extra Medium",
     hero="collage-extra",
     q="How do local businesses get more customers?",
     desc="The local playbook: Google Business Profile and local search, reviews that do the persuading, local creators and community presence, and offers people can act on today.",
     answer="Local businesses win customers by being findable at the exact moment someone nearby is ready to buy: a complete Google Business Profile, strong local search presence, a steady flow of real reviews, and a visible life in the neighborhood &mdash; local creators, community presence, and offers a person can act on today, not someday.",
     sections=[
       ("Be the obvious answer in local search", "<p>Most local purchases start as a search with intent: a service, a neighborhood, &ldquo;near me.&rdquo; The business that wins that moment is rarely the best business in town; it is the most findable one. That starts with a Google Business Profile treated like a storefront, not a form you filled out once: correct categories, the full service list, current hours, real photos, and posts that show the place is alive. Your website&rsquo;s job in local search is confirmation &mdash; same name, same details, a clear page for each service and area, so the searcher who found you in the map feels they have landed in the right place.</p>"),
       ("Reviews are the local word of mouth", "<p>Buyers do not read your claims; they read your customers. A steady flow of recent reviews beats a large pile from three years ago, because recency reads as a business that is still good this month. Ask at the moment of satisfaction, make it one tap, and respond to every review like the public conversation it is &mdash; the responses persuade the next hundred readers, not the one reviewer. Handled this way, reviews quietly answer the questions buyers actually have: Are they reliable? Are they worth the money? What happens when something goes wrong?</p>"),
       ("Be visibly part of the neighborhood", "<p>Local demand also follows local life. The restaurants, shops and services people recommend are the ones they keep encountering: the local creator who actually eats there, the team on the community board, the business that shows up at the neighborhood&rsquo;s events instead of only in its feed. Offers close the loop &mdash; something specific a nearby person can act on today, not a vague promise to keep you in mind. Our lab built <a href=\"/labs/magpie/\">Magpie</a> for the creator part of this playbook, and the full local-creator approach is laid out in <a href=\"/influencer-marketing-for-local-businesses/\">influencer marketing for local businesses</a>.</p>"),
     ],
     faqs=[
       ("How long does local search take to pay off?", "Profile fixes and corrections can show results in weeks. Rankings and review volume compound over months of consistency. Local search rewards the business that keeps its presence current, which is why starting now beats starting perfectly."),
       ("Do we need a big social media following?", "No. Local buyers need to find you, trust you and reach you, in that order. A modest, current presence that answers real questions beats a large, stale following that cannot walk through your door."),
       ("How do we start with Extra Medium?", "In writing, through the contact page. Tell us where your customers come from now and where they should. The first conversation maps which part of the local playbook is missing for your business, and what we would fix first."),
     ],
     takeaways=[
       "The most findable business wins the moment, not always the best one.",
       "Recent reviews are this month&rsquo;s word of mouth.",
       "Local customers come from local presence: search, reviews, creators, community.",
     ],
     keys=["local marketing", "Google Business Profile", "local SEO", "customer reviews", "local customers", "near me search", "community marketing"],
     iso="2026-11-15"),
dict(slug="are-paid-ads-worth-it-for-small-business", title="Are Paid Ads Worth It for a Small Business? | Extra Medium",
     hero="collage-labs",
     q="Are paid ads worth it for a small business?",
     desc="When paid advertising works, when it quietly burns money, the three numbers to know before spending, and how paid and organic should be sequenced.",
     answer="Paid ads are worth it when three things are true: the margin on a new customer covers the cost of buying one, people are already searching for what you sell, and someone follows up fast when interest arrives. Miss any one of the three and ads do not fail loudly &mdash; they just quietly burn the budget.",
     sections=[
       ("Run the math before you spend", "<p>Three numbers decide whether ads can work for you. The <b>margin</b> on a new customer: what one sale actually contributes after the costs of delivering it. The <b>value over time</b>: whether that customer comes back, refers others, or is gone forever. And your <b>close rate</b>: of the people who raise a hand, how many become customers. The arithmetic is simple. If a new customer is worth $400 in margin and you close one lead in four, a lead can cost up to $100 before the math breaks &mdash; and you would rather it cost half that. If your real numbers make a lead worth $12, no platform, targeting trick or agency can save the campaign. The math is the strategy.</p>"),
       ("When paid works, and when it burns", "<p>Paid works when it intercepts existing demand: people already searching for the thing, an offer worth clicking, a page that continues the promise the ad made, and follow-up measured in minutes. Paid burns when it is asked to manufacture demand for a weak offer, when margins are too thin to buy attention at auction prices, when traffic lands on a homepage that says nothing specific, or when leads sit unanswered until the interest is cold. Notice that none of the failure modes live inside the ad account. The platform gets blamed for arithmetic and follow-up that were broken before the first dollar was spent.</p>"),
       ("Sequence: earn it, then amplify it", "<p>The order matters. First, fix the offer and the follow-up, because ads multiply whatever is already true about your business &mdash; including the leaks. Second, build the free foundation: the local presence and search visibility in <a href=\"/how-local-businesses-get-more-customers/\">how local businesses get more customers</a>, which keeps producing after the budget pauses. Third, use paid to pour volume into what already converts, and to test offers faster than organic reach allows. Paid and organic are not rivals; paid is the accelerator, not the engine. <a href=\"/services/\">The studio&rsquo;s Grow practice</a> runs them in that order for exactly this reason.</p>"),
     ],
     faqs=[
       ("How much should a small business spend on ads?", "There is no standard number, and anyone who gives you one before asking about your margins is guessing with your money. Start with a test budget sized by the math: enough to buy real data on your cost per lead, small enough that the lesson is affordable either way."),
       ("Should we start with search ads or social ads?", "If people are actively searching for what you sell, search captures that demand first. Social interrupts rather than answers, so it suits visual offers, local experiences and creating demand that does not exist yet. The right order follows where the intent already lives."),
       ("How do we start with Extra Medium?", "In writing, through the contact page. Bring your three numbers if you have them: margin, repeat value and close rate. The first conversation runs the math with you and says plainly whether paid can work for your business yet."),
     ],
     takeaways=[
       "Ads multiply whatever is already true about your business, including the leaks.",
       "The math is the strategy: margin, lifetime value, close rate.",
       "Fix the offer and the follow-up first. Then buy volume.",
     ],
     keys=["paid ads", "small business advertising", "Google Ads", "cost per lead", "ad spend", "paid vs organic", "search advertising"],
     iso="2026-11-15"),
dict(slug="influencer-marketing-for-local-businesses", title="Influencer Marketing for Local Businesses | Extra Medium",
     hero="collage-home",
     q="Does influencer marketing work for local businesses?",
     desc="Why local creators beat celebrity reach for local businesses, how comped experiences compare with paid posts, and how to check local reach before you book.",
     answer="Yes, when the influencer is actually local. A creator whose followers live in your trade area can fill tables, book appointments and move product in a way a celebrity post never will, because their audience can act on it today. The discipline is checking local reach before you book &mdash; not follower count, which measures the wrong map.",
     sections=[
       ("Why local beats famous for a local business", "<p>Every audience has a geography. A celebrity&rsquo;s million followers are spread across every time zone, which means almost none of them can book your table, your chair or your service this week. A local creator&rsquo;s audience is concentrated where your customers live, and it comes with a second advantage money cannot buy at national scale: trust. Their followers know their streets, their tastes and their honesty, because the creator&rsquo;s life looks like theirs. For a business that sells within a few miles, a smaller audience that can actually walk in beats a famous one that can only watch.</p>"),
       ("Comped experience or paid post?", "<p>The two standard arrangements buy different things. A <b>comped experience</b> trades the product itself &mdash; the meal, the service, the visit &mdash; for the creator&rsquo;s coverage of it. It suits restaurants, salons, attractions and services where the experience is the story, and it keeps the post honest, because the creator genuinely had it. A <b>paid post</b> buys defined deliverables at the creator&rsquo;s rate: specific posts, specific timing, a launch or campaign that needs control. Either way, agree the deliverables, the timing and how the content may be reused, in writing, before anything is posted. Handshakes are how good intentions turn into mismatched expectations.</p>"),
       ("Check the map before you book", "<p>Follower count measures the wrong map. Before booking anyone, look at where their audience actually is: ask for the geography behind the account, read the comments for local names and local references, and weigh engagement from your area over raw totals. This discipline is why our lab built <a href=\"/labs/magpie/\">Magpie</a>, a local creator platform that shows each creator&rsquo;s local reach &mdash; the share of their audience that actually lives in your market &mdash; before anyone books, so a business can choose creators by the audience that can act, not the biggest number on the profile. Magpie is one of the products in development and live in <a href=\"/labs/\">Extra Medium Labs</a>, where the studio tests its own advice with its own money first.</p>"),
     ],
     faqs=[
       ("How much do local creators charge?", "It varies by creator and by what is being asked. At the local level, many creators happily work for a comped experience; established local voices list a rate for paid posts with defined deliverables. The right comparison is cost against the customers their local audience can actually send you."),
       ("How do we find creators in our area?", "Start with who already posts about your category in your town, then check the geography of their audience before their follower count. Platforms like Magpie exist to make that check the first step instead of an afterthought."),
       ("How do we start with Extra Medium?", "In writing, through the contact page. Tell us what you sell and the area you sell it in. The first conversation covers whether local creators fit your business, which arrangement to use, and what a first booking should look like."),
     ],
     takeaways=[
       "Follower count measures the wrong map. Local reach is the number that spends.",
       "A local audience can act today. A famous one can only watch.",
       "Comp the experience when the experience is the story. Pay when you need control.",
     ],
     keys=["influencer marketing", "local creators", "local reach", "Magpie", "comped experiences", "creator marketing", "local influencers"],
     iso="2026-11-15"),
]
